    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hey, my realtor just mentioned something about needing to set up an escrow account for my house purchase here in Toronto. I've never dealt with this before, can you explain what escrow actually is and why it's used during the closing process?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Think of escrow as a neutral third-party holding pen for your money and documents during the closing process. It's like a referee who holds the ball until both teams are ready to play. In Toronto, your realtor is likely referring to a deposit held by a lawyer or trust company, not a monthly mortgage escrow (which is more common in the U.S. for taxes and insurance). The escrow account protects both you and the seller: your deposit isn't handed over until the seller meets all conditions, and the seller knows the funds are serious and committed. It's a safeguard against either side backing out or failing to deliver on promises.",
      "source": "target",
